WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.740
A temporary file should be created with permissions appropriate for its contents.

2
00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:14.760
If the file contains intermediate data, reports, parsed records, or operational details, the default should be restrictive access rather than broad readability.

3
00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.760
Ownership expectations should be explicit.

4
00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:25.940
A per-user tool, a service account, a privileged helper, and a container process may all create files under different identities.

5
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:31.960
Review who owns the directory, who owns the file, and which other processes can read or modify it.

6
00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:33.920
Inherited permissions matter.

7
00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:42.100
On some systems, process settings, directory defaults, or platform-specific inheritance can affect the final access mode.

8
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:47.880
A secure design does not assume that a developer workstation default will match production behavior.

9
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.520
Minimize sensitive data before it reaches disk.

10
00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:59.060
Avoid writing secrets, tokens, raw personal data, large contents, and unnecessary diagnostics into temporary files.

11
00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:06.120
If sensitive material is unavoidable, the decision should be driven by policy and reviewed as part of the system design.

12
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:08.960
Temporary secrets are still secrets.

13
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:17.540
A value written for only a few milliseconds may be captured by backups, crash dumps, endpoint tools, debug bundles, or support scripts.

14
00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:24.920
If the system can stream, keep data in memory, or write a less sensitive representation, consider that design first.

15
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.240
Encryption or alternative storage may be required in some environments, but it should not be treated as a universal cleanup substitute.

16
00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:37.860
Permissions, lifetime, access control, and deletion behavior still need review.

17
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:50.620
A practical review asks: what data is written, who can read it, how long it remains, what defaults affect access, and whether the same operational goal could be achieved with less sensitive content.

18
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.700
Developers should also consider derived data.

19
00:01:53.800 --> 00:02:03.560
A report fragment, transformed record, or extracted archive member may still reveal sensitive facts even if it is not a direct copy of the original input.

20
00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:06.460
Minimize and classify those contents too.
